.TH SYS_MMAP 2
.SH NAME
sys_mmap, sys_munmap, sys_mprotect \- memory given on first touch (SYS_MMAP, SYS_MUNMAP, SYS_MPROTECT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

void *sys_mmap(void *addr, size_t size, int flags);
long sys_munmap(void *addr, size_t size, int flags);
long sys_mprotect(void *addr, size_t size, int prot);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_mmap
reserves
.I size
bytes (rounded up to 4 KiB pages) of anonymous memory in the calling
process. Nothing is given yet: each page is given, zeroed, when first
touched. Without
.B MAP_FIXED
in
.I flags
the kernel chooses where (the first gap that fits, above 16 TiB;
.I addr
is ignored); with it, exactly at
.I addr
(page-aligned), replacing what was there.
.PP
The rights, or-ed into
.I flags
and
.I prot
(Linux's values):
.BR PROT_READ ,
.BR PROT_WRITE ,
.BR PROT_EXEC ;
.B PROT_NONE
(0) gives no access at all (guard pages). Writable and executable
together is allowed on anonymous memory (a JIT's code pool); programs'
own files stay W^X.
.PP
.B sys_munmap
removes the memory in the range, and its pages; with
.B UNMAP_KEEP
in
.I flags
it only gives the pages back, and they come back zeroed when touched
(MADV_DONTNEED). Shared memory keeps its contents.
.B sys_mprotect
gives new rights to memory that
.B sys_mmap
gave; every page of the range must be reserved.
.PP
Pages count in the process's memory quota when they are given.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sys_mmap :
the address, or a negative error (cast to a pointer). The others: 0, or
a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I size
is 0 or too big, an unknown flag, or an address that is not page-aligned
or outside the area for such memory.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No gap big enough, no kernel memory, or
.RB ( sys_mprotect )
part of the range is not reserved.
.SH NOTES
Touching a page beyond the quota or free memory is a fault, as a bad
address is: the process ends (the kernel logs "out of memory").
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_brk (2),
.BR sys_shm_create (2),
.BR sys_futex (2)
